    "case_id": "420",
    "case_summary": "A patient in their 20s without noteworthy medical history presented to the emergency department with chest pain and dyspnea. The patient experienced palpitations and symptoms of upper respiratory tract infection 3 days prior.\n\nVital signs:- Temperature: 36.5°C- Heart rate: 88 beats per minute - Respiratory rate: 22 breaths per minute- Blood pressure: 105/70 mm Hg- Oxygen saturation: 89% on room airPatient lost consciousness 20 minutes after arrival, with blood pressure decreasing to 67/43 mm Hg\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Troponin T: 4.74 μg/L (normal: 0-0.03 μg/L)- Pro-brain natriuretic peptide: 4850 pg/mL (normal: <300 pg/mL)- C-reactive protein: 18.50 mg/L (normal: 0-6 mg/dL)- Procalcitonin: 39.68 ng/mL (normal: 0-0.05 ng/mL)Imaging Studies:- Transthoracic echocardiography: Severe left ventricular dysfunction (ejection fraction 38%), wall motion abnormalities in multiple vascular territories- Coronary angiography: Normal coronary arteriesECG Findings:Initial ECG (Figure A): Upward QRS complex in lead aVR and pure R wave in leads V1 and V2Follow-up ECG (Figure B): Sinus tachycardia (105 beats/min); low QRS voltage in limb leads; ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, aVF, and V1-V6; ST-segment depression in leads aVR and aVL",
